"Upper Bounds for Sequence Saturation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.17683)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Authors: Shihan Kanungo ... In this paper, we study the saturation function $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)$ for sequences. ... We present an algorithm that constructs a $u$-saturated sequence on $n$ letters and apply it to show $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)=O(n)$ for several families of sequences $u$, including all repetitions of the form $abcabc\\dots$. ... Finally, we describe a linear program that computes the exact value of $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)$ for arbitrary $n$ and $u$.\nTitle: Upper Bounds for Sequence Saturation\nAuthors: Shihan Kanungo\nDate: Fri Dec 19 15:19:26 2025\n\nIn this paper, we study the saturation function $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)$ for sequences. Saturation for sequences was introduced by Anand, Geneson, Kaustav, and Tsai (2021), who proved that $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)=O(n)$ for two-letter sequences $u$ and conjectured that this bound holds for all sequences. We present an algorithm that constructs a $u$-saturated sequence on $n$ letters and apply it to show $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)=O(n)$ for several families of sequences $u$, including all repetitions of the form $abcabc\\dots$. We further establish $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)=O(n)$ for a broad class of sequences of the form $aa\\dots bb$. In addition, we prove that for most sequences $u$, there exists an infinite $u$-saturated sequence. For three-letter sequences of the form $abc\\dots xyz$, where $a,b,c$ are distinct and $xyz$ is a permutation of $abc$, we show -- under certain structural assumptions on $u$ -- that $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)=O(n)$. Finally, we describe a linear program that computes the exact value of $\\mathrm{Sat}(n,u)$ for arbitrary $n$ and $u$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUPPER BOUNDS FOR SEQUENCE SATURATION (https://math.mit.edu/research/highschool/primes/materials/2025/Kanungo.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; SHIHAN KANUNGO ... In this paper, we study the saturation function Sat(n, u) for sequences. ... We present an algorithm that constructs a u-saturated sequence on ... Well-known examples include Ramsey theory and the forbidden subgraph problem. ... nally introduced to analyze linear differential equations ([6]), Davenport-Schinzel sequences\nUPPER BOUNDS FOR SEQUENCE SATURATION\nSHIHAN KANUNGO\nAbstract. In this paper, we study the saturation function Sat(n, u) for sequences. Sat-\nuration for sequences was introduced by Anand, Geneson, Kaustav, and Tsai (2021), who\nproved that Sat(n, u) = O(n) for two-letter sequences u and conjectured that this bound\nholds for all sequences. We present an algorithm that constructs a u-saturated sequence on\nn letters and apply it to show Sat(n, u) = O(n) for several families of sequences u, including\nall repetitions of the form abcabc . . . . We further establish Sat(n, u) = O(n) for a broad class\nof sequences of the form aa . . . bb. In addition, we prove that for most sequences u, there\nexists an infinite u-saturated sequence. For three-letter sequences of the form abc . . . xyz,\nwhere a, b, c are distinct and xyz is a permutation of abc, we show—under certain struc-\ntural assumptions on u—that Sat(n, u) = O(n). Finally, we describe a linear program that\ncomputes the exact value of Sat(n, u) for arbitrary n and u.\n1. Introduction\nThe concept of pattern avoidance lies at the heart of extremal combinatorics, which in-\nvestigates questions such as:\nWhat is the largest possible structure that avoids a given substructure?\nWell-known examples include Ramsey theory and the forbidden subgraph problem. Another\nimportant example is the study of Davenport-Schinzel sequences, which are sequences\nS over an alphabet of n distinct letters that satisfy the following constraints:\n• For any two distinct letters a, b, the alternating subsequence abab . . . of length\ns + 2 is forbidden, where s is a nonnegative integer.\n• No two consecutive letters in S are the same.\nA key quantity of interest is the function λs(n), which denotes the length of the longest\nDavenport-Schinzel sequence over an n-letter alphabet. For s ≥3, λs(n) grows as n times a\nsmall, but nonconstant, factor. For instance,\nλ3(n) = Θ(nα(n)),\nwhere α(n) is the inverse Ackermann function1, known for its extremely slow growth. Origi-\nnally introduced to analyze linear differential equations ([6]), Davenport-Schinzel sequences\nand the function λs(n) have since found numerous applications in discrete geometry and\ngeometric algorithms ([2]), as well as application to the saturation function for 0–1 matrices\n([12]).\nA generalized Davenport-Schinzel sequence extends this concept by requiring se-\nquences to avoid a given pattern u on r distinct letters while also being r-sparse, meaning\nDate: December 20, 2025.\n2020 Mathematics Subject Classification. 05D99.\nKey words and phrases. Sequence Saturation, Generalized Davenport-Schinzel Sequences.\n1see [13] for a definition\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPattern Avoidance in Sequences (https://math.mit.edu/research/highschool/primes/materials/2025/October/8-2-Kanungo.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Algorithm | | --- | --- | --- Consider the following algorithm: 1: Input: Alphabet A = {1, . . . , n}, forbidden sequence u 2: Output: u-saturated sequence 3: Initialize the sequence: s ←1, 2, . . . , r −1 ▷ Initial sequence avoids u 4: while it is possible to extend the sequence do 5: for each letter x ∈A do 6: if x can be properly inserted into s then 7: Insert x appropriately into s to form s′ ▷ Smallest x, leftmost position 8: Update s ←s′ ▷ New sequence 9: break ▷ Exit loop after the first valid insertion 10: end if 11: end for 12: end while 13: Return s ▷ Final sequence is u-saturated | | Shihan Kanungo (SJSU) | Sequence Saturation | Sunday, October 19, 2025 14 / 22\nAlgorithm | | -