 of relatively prime integers with $p, q \\geq 3$ for which there is no infinite ternary square-free word whose subsequences modulo $p$ and $q$ are square-free. To prove our result, we combine different techniques, including the construction of words from multi-valued square-free morphisms and circular square-free morphisms. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnumeration of tilings of benzels (after Colin Defant, Rupert Li, Jim Propp and Ben Young) (https://mathematik.univie.ac.at/eventsnews/nachrichtenvolldarstellung/news/enumeration-of-tilings-of-benzels-after-colin-defant-rupert-li-jim-propp-and-ben-young/?cHash=7853555c72438ac04627be95c7fec297&no_cache=1)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Enumeration of tilings of benzels (after Colin Defant, Rupert Li, Jim Propp and Ben Young)\n\nAG Diskrete Mathematik | 15.11.2022 15:15 - 16:45 ... Their solution contains several interesting ideas, including the application of the classical k-quotient bijection for partitions and a new \"compress\" bijection.\n\n# \n\n# Enumeration of tilings of benzels (after Colin Defant, Rupert Li, Jim Propp and Ben Young)\n\nAG Diskrete Mathematik | 15.11.2022 15:15 - 16:45\n\nChristian Krattenthaler (U Wien)\n\nAbstract: In an article from this summer, Jim Propp has considered tilings of regions consisting of hexagons. He formulated twenty mostly open problems (his formulation) on the enumeration of such tilings.\nTwo of these conjectures were recently solved by Defant, Li, Propp and Young. Their solution contains several interesting ideas, including the application of the classical k-quotient bijection for partitions and a new \"compress\" bijection. Clearly, there still remain many open problems ... \n\nOrganiser:\n\nCh. Krattenthaler\n\nLocation:\n\nTU Wien, Wiedner Hauptstrasze 8-10, Institut fuer Diskrete Mathematik und Geometrie, Dissertantenzimmer, 8. Stock, Turm A-------------------